--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -2,6 +2,24 @@
 
 SRE-bench (Software Reverse Engineering Benchmark) is a reverse-engineering benchmark. It runs coding agents against real, compiled programs inside a locked-down Docker sandbox and scores how well they reverse-engineer behavior, formats, protocols, and devices. It spans five RE domains, each backed by a held-out component that makes grading deterministic, and every challenge is scored from 0 to 6 with partial credit. Each domain's program is compiled into many build variants so the same task can be studied across a controlled range of readability: 19 unique programs across the five domains, 346 total challenge cells.
 
+## Local crackmes control family
+
+The local `crackmes` extension adds two cells beyond the core matrix:
+`crackmes/c-noopt-nosym-static` and
+`crackmes/c-noopt-nosym-static-sre-obf-max`. Both are matched C/O0/static/stripped
+builds of one synthetic password checker; the latter uses the modified sre-obf
+native IR preset. This small control is intended for cheap obfuscation experiments.
+
+The agent statically recovers an eight-character flag and writes
+`/workspace/submission/flag.txt`. `crackmes_exact` scores **0 or 1**, with no partial
+credit and no submitted-code execution. The prompt explains owner authorization
+and the synthetic setting. Static-only compliance needs trace review; the current
+harness does not technically prevent target execution or emulation.
+
+Both cells support the usual run options and `--verify`. See
+[the family README](binaries/crackmes/README.md) for build settings, grader rules,
+private artifact layout, reproducible integration sources, and example commands.
+
 ## Domains
 
 For each domain, **Target** is what the software does, **RE Task** is what the agent must accomplish, **Infrastructure** is the held-out component that makes evaluation deterministic, and **Auxiliary** is what the agent receives beyond the binary.
